4. JPA VS Hibernate:
JPA is a standard for ORM, object relational mapping, it maps java objects to database tables and perform
database operations without writing queries.
Hibernate is an implementation of JPA, but it extends JPA, adding some new features that are helpful. Besides
Hibernate, there are other implementations of JPA

5. Hiraki Pool and benefits?
Hiraki pool is a connection pool. Since connect a disconnect with a database is expensive, Hiraki maintain
several open connections so low latency and high performance

6. @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, @ManyToMany
Posts - comments : one To many
comments - posts: many to one
students - courses: many to many

7. Cascade
Cascade = CascadeType.All: it defines how database change when the related database change. All means all
CRUD operations will make changes to the child database
OrphanRemoval = true: remove the child entity if it no longer belong to the parent, like a review belongs
to a product, if the review is removed from the collection, we delete it from the database

8. fetch = FetchType.LAZY, fetch = FetchType.EAGER
It defines how the jpa load related entities. Lazy is until we actually access the entry in our code, them
it loads, eager is we load all related entries beforehand. If we need data immediately and the data is small
we can use eager, or we may want lazy to improve the performance.

9. JPA naming conventions?
We don't need to write our owm queries to interact with database, the jpa will generate queries according to
the methods names. There are some built-in methods but we can also create our own. For example,
findByFirstnameAndLastname, findByFirstnameOrLastname, findByAgeIn

13. JPQL?
A query language to query java objects.

14. @NamedQuery and @NamedQueries
Single jpql VS several jpqls

15. @Query
Different from @NamedQuery, @NamedQuery is defined in entity class level, but the @Query is defined in repository
interface method level. Both of them are queries for java objects.

17. EntityManager
A core interface in JPA to Manage CRUD for entities. In spring boot we usually don't explicitly use it,
spring data jpa handles it for us.

18. Session factory and session?
They are concepts of Hibernate. Hibernate has a session factory to create sessions to interact with database.
Session factory is created once per application. It uses Hikari pool to get jdbc connections.
Session factory manages the lifecycle of session, when we want to process the data, a new session is created,
and the session shut down when everything complete. Session factory is thread safe while session is not.

19. Transaction
A series of operations for database, if any operation fail, the database will roll back. Only when everything
be done successfully, the database will be updated. We can mark a method as @Transactional to make a transaction
method. Transactional works with @EnableTransactionManager, but we don't have to manually add this annotation
in spring boot app.

20. Hibernate caching
We have first level cache and second level cache. The first level cache exists in a single session and it is
default. When we want to fetch data, hibernate would check first level cache. When the session close the cache
no longer exists. Second level cache is not default, we need to configure it manually in the application. properties.
The cache is share across sessions. The cache exists long time.




